add('small-desk-setup','A small desk setup that leaves room to work','Desk setup','Plan a compact desk around your actual tasks, with a footprint test, storage trade-offs and a no-spend starting point.','small desk organizer','デスク 収納 コンパクト', '''
<p>A small desk does not need a smaller version of every accessory. It needs fewer objects competing for the same space. Start with your busiest ordinary task: a laptop open beside a notebook, a mouse moving freely, and a phone charging without its cable crossing your writing area.</p>
<h2>Do the paper footprint test</h2><p>Measure the usable surface, including the space lost to a monitor base or rear cable gap. Cut paper rectangles for the laptop, keyboard, mouse area and open notebook. Arrange them before ordering an organizer. Leave the chair in its usual position: a layout that works only when you stretch around a shelf is not a useful layout.</p>
<p>For an illustrative 90 × 50 cm desk, a shelf across the entire rear edge may consume the only place an open laptop fits. A narrow vertical file holder at one side may be more useful. These are planning dimensions, not universal ergonomic targets; test your own equipment.</p>
<h2>Choose one way to reclaim space</h2><table><tr><th>Option</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Check first</th></tr><tr><td>Monitor riser</td><td>A keyboard can slide beneath it when unused</td><td>Internal clearance and base footprint</td></tr><tr><td>Clamp accessory</td><td>The rear desk edge is accessible</td><td>Top thickness, underside frame and clamp limits</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical laptop holder</td><td>You work with an external display</td><td>Closed-display support and ventilation</td></tr></table>
<h2>Keep the center deliberately empty</h2><p>Put daily tools within easy reach and move occasional supplies into a drawer or nearby box. Use the back edge for cable routing, but leave slack for moving the laptop. Avoid turning the front edge into a row of docks and containers. The clearest area should be where your hands work, not a decorative corner.</p>
<h2>A purchase order that avoids clutter</h2><ol><li>Remove objects unused during a normal week.</li><li>Try a temporary arrangement with existing boxes.</li><li>Identify one remaining problem, such as a cable falling behind the desk.</li><li>Buy only the accessory that solves that problem.</li></ol><p>In Japan, listings often use centimetres; US listings may use inches. Convert the entire footprint, including feet and clamps. Check whether a pictured shelf, light or chair is included in the package rather than assuming the staged photograph shows one complete set.</p>
<h2>When to skip the upgrade</h2><p>If an organizer merely stores objects you do not use, clearing them out is the cheaper fix. Keep the receipt and test a new accessory with a full work session before discarding packaging. A useful small-desk upgrade creates working space without adding another obstacle.</p>''')
add('usb-c-cable-guide','USB-C cables: match power, data and display','Charging & cables','A practical USB-C buying checklist that separates charging wattage, data speed, display support and cable length.','usb c cable power data','USB C ケーブル 充電 データ', '''
<p>Two USB-C cables can fit the same socket and still do different jobs. Shopping by connector shape alone is an easy way to buy a cable that charges a laptop but will not perform the transfer or display task you bought it for.</p>
<h2>Write down the job before the specification</h2><p>Choose the main task: charging a phone, powering a laptop, moving large files, or connecting a display. Then check the specifications of both devices. A cable cannot add a display-output feature to a port that does not support it.</p>
<table><tr><th>Your task</th><th>Look for</th><th>Do not assume</th></tr><tr><td>Phone charging</td><td>Correct connectors and a suitable power rating</td><td>A more expensive data cable charges faster</td></tr><tr><td>Laptop charging</td><td>Cable rating and charger output suited to the computer</td><td>Every USB-C charger powers every laptop adequately</td></tr><tr><td>External storage</td><td>An explicit data rate supported by both endpoints</td><td>Wattage tells you transfer speed</td></tr><tr><td>External monitor</td><td>Documented display support across the whole connection</td><td>A charging cable necessarily carries video</td></tr></table>
<h2>Keep power and speed separate</h2><p>Read the power rating and data rate as separate specifications. USB-IF describes distinct power and performance markings for certified cables. A power number on the package is not a promise about file-transfer speed. Look for a precise manufacturer specification instead of treating words such as “premium” or “fast” as technical standards.</p>
<h2>Buy the length you can actually use</h2><p>Measure the path from charger to device with the device in its normal position. Include the bend around a desk edge and enough slack to lift the phone. An extra-long cable can create a loop under your chair; a very short one can pull against a socket. Verify the capabilities of the exact length and variant you select, rather than another option on the same listing.</p>
<h2>A simple two-cable strategy</h2><p>If your needs differ, label a desk charging cable and a separate display or data cable. This is often easier than expecting every cable in the house to perform every task. Keep the label near a connector where you can see it without unplugging the setup.</p>
<h2>Before checkout</h2><ul><li>Confirm both connector ends, not just “USB-C” in the title.</li><li>Check your device manufacturer's power and display requirements.</li><li>Compare exact model numbers across US and Japan listings.</li><li>Read the warranty and seller details for the store you use.</li></ul><p>Do not replace a working cable just to chase a larger wattage number. Replace it when its documented capabilities do not meet a real need, or when it is damaged. Stop using frayed or visibly damaged cables.</p>''',[('USB-IF: cables and connectors','https://www.usb.org/cable_connector')])
add('phone-stand-guide','Phone stands: stability before extra features','Phone accessories','Choose a desk phone stand by case fit, tapping stability, charging clearance and viewing position instead of decorative features.','adjustable phone stand desk','スマホ スタンド 卓上 調整', '''
<p>A phone stand should hold your actual phone, in its actual case, at the place you use it. A stand that looks tidy in a product photo may wobble every time you tap a message or hide the charging port behind a support lip.</p>
<h2>Choose for your main routine</h2><p>For video calls, prioritize a position that keeps the camera unobstructed and a base that stays put. For following a recipe or reference document, landscape support and angle adjustment may matter more. For checking notifications, a simple fixed stand may be enough. Avoid paying for a charging feature when your existing cable already solves the problem.</p>
<table><tr><th>Design</th><th>Practical advantage</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr><tr><td>Fixed cradle</td><td>Few moving parts and a predictable position</td><td>Limited angle adjustment</td></tr><tr><td>Folding adjustable stand</td><td>Easy to pack and reposition</td><td>Hinges and base stability need checking</td></tr><tr><td>Magnetic stand</td><td>Quick attachment with a compatible phone or case</td><td>Compatibility and charging are separate questions</td></tr></table>
<h2>Read the dimensions that photographs hide</h2><p>Compare cradle depth with the phone and case together. A thick protective case, attached wallet or grip can change the fit. Check whether the lower supports block gesture navigation, speakers or the connector. If you charge in landscape orientation, verify where the cable exits and whether it reaches without pulling the stand.</p>
<h2>Magnetic does not automatically mean charging</h2><p>A magnetic holder may contain no charging electronics. A wireless charging stand may need a separate adapter. Read the package contents and the manufacturer's compatibility list. Do not treat a similar-looking magnetic ring as proof of a particular charging standard or speed.</p>
<h2>Run a practical return-window test</h2><ol><li>Fit the phone with the case you normally use.</li><li>Tap near each upper corner and check for rocking.</li><li>Connect your cable and move the phone into both orientations.</li><li>Take a short call and check framing, microphone clearance and screen reflections.</li></ol><p>Test on your desk surface: a non-slip base can behave differently on a cloth mat than on a smooth tabletop. A taller arm also gives a tap more leverage, so the highest adjustment is not always the most usable setting.</p>
<h2>US and Japan shopping notes</h2><p>Search by dimensions and supported device size rather than only a phone model name. Listings may bundle a cable in one marketplace and omit it in another. Compare the exact package, local return terms and delivery date. Keep a working stand if the only benefit of a new one is matching the desk colour.</p>''')
add('desk-lighting','Desk lighting without screen glare','Desk setup','Place a desk lamp around your screen, writing hand and room light, then choose a clamp or base that fits.','adjustable desk lamp','デスクライト 調光', '''
<p>The most useful desk lamp is not necessarily the brightest listing. It is the one that puts light on your task without shining into your eyes or reflecting across the screen. Try moving an existing lamp before deciding that you need a new one.</p>
<h2>Find the reflection first</h2><p>Sit at your usual position with the screen at a normal working angle. Move the lamp and watch for reflections on the display. Then open a notebook and check whether your writing hand casts a shadow over the page. These two observations tell you more about placement than a photograph of someone else's desk.</p>
<h2>Compare attachment styles</h2><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Best reason to consider it</th><th>Measure before ordering</th></tr><tr><td>Base lamp</td><td>You need to move the light frequently</td><td>Base footprint and arm reach</td></tr><tr><td>Clamp lamp</td><td>Surface space is scarce</td><td>Clamp opening, frame clearance and top material</td></tr><tr><td>Monitor-mounted light</td><td>You want the desktop beneath it clear</td><td>Monitor shape, mounting limits and webcam position</td></tr></table>
<h2>Useful controls beat feature lists</h2><p>A reachable brightness control is valuable if you switch between a monitor and paper. An adjustable arm helps when you change tasks. Colour-temperature controls are optional; judge whether the range feels useful in your room rather than treating more modes as automatically better. Check whether controls remember their settings after power is disconnected if you use a switched outlet.</p>
<h2>Watch what the bundle requires</h2><p>Some compact lights use USB power and do not include an adapter. Others have a dedicated mains supply. Verify the power source, cable length and included parts. For cross-border purchases, check the input rating and plug on the supplied adapter; a familiar product name does not guarantee the same regional bundle.</p>
<h2>A placement experiment before buying</h2><ol><li>Clear the proposed mounting point.</li><li>Try lighting from either side while writing.</li><li>Check the screen with both light and dark content.</li><li>Use the setup with your usual room lighting.</li><li>Choose only the adjustment range you found necessary.</li></ol><p>A monitor light is not a reason to work in a completely dark room, and no lamp can guarantee relief from discomfort. If a lighting change does not solve a persistent problem, do not keep adding brighter accessories as a substitute for appropriate advice.</p>
<h2>When a simple lamp wins</h2><p>A basic lamp with enough reach and a stable base can outperform an elaborate model that cannot mount to your desk. Spend on a fit you can verify. Skip an upgrade if repositioning the current light removes the reflection and leaves the writing area clear.</p>''')
add('cable-management','Cable management you can still unplug','Desk setup','Organize desk cables with service loops, removable ties and a practical split between fixed and frequently moved devices.','reusable cable ties desk clips','ケーブル クリップ 結束 バンド', '''
<p>A tidy desk becomes frustrating when every cable is tied so tightly that unplugging a laptop requires dismantling the arrangement. Good cable management keeps connections reachable and leaves enough movement for the devices you pick up.</p>
<h2>Separate fixed and moving equipment</h2><p>Make two groups. Fixed equipment includes a monitor or lamp that rarely moves. Moving equipment includes a laptop, phone or headphones. Route the fixed cables along the rear edge. Give the moving cables their own accessible path instead of trapping them inside a single dense bundle.</p>
<table><tr><th>Accessory</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Common mistake</th></tr><tr><td>Reusable ties</td><td>Loose groups that may change</td><td>Pulling them tight against connectors</td></tr><tr><td>Edge clips</td><td>Keeping charging ends within reach</td><td>Choosing a groove that does not fit the cable</td></tr><tr><td>Under-desk tray</td><td>Supporting suitable adapters and spare length</td><td>Blocking knee space or covering ventilation</td></tr><tr><td>Labels</td><td>Identifying both ends of similar leads</td><td>Labelling only the end you already recognize</td></tr></table>
<h2>Leave a deliberate movement loop</h2><p>Place the laptop where you normally work, then lift it into the position used for unplugging or docking. Keep enough spare length for that movement without pulling at the socket. Support a heavy adapter separately; do not let its weight hang from a device's port.</p>
<h2>Protect the desk finish</h2><p>Adhesive clips can mark or lift some finishes. Check the furniture and accessory instructions, and try an inconspicuous location when appropriate. A removable adhesive is not a universal guarantee of residue-free removal. Clamp-on options also need a sound, compatible surface and adequate clearance underneath.</p>
<h2>Work from devices toward the outlet</h2><ol><li>Turn equipment off when required by its instructions before rearranging power connections.</li><li>Label the monitor, charger and peripheral leads.</li><li>Set device positions and movement slack.</li><li>Group remaining length loosely with reusable ties.</li><li>Check the full chair movement and any desk height adjustment.</li></ol><p>Keep mains equipment accessible and ventilated, follow its load limits, and never use decorative cable boxes as an excuse to hide damaged connections. Do not daisy-chain power strips.</p>
<h2>Buy a small quantity first</h2><p>One pack of well-sized clips is more useful than a large mixed kit full of parts that do not fit. Measure cable thickness and the mounting surface before ordering. Japanese search terms such as ケーブルクリップ help find local options; compare dimensions rather than relying on the photograph. Revisit the routing after a week and remove any accessory that makes routine changes harder.</p>''')
add('laptop-stand','Laptop stands: plan the keyboard as well','Desk setup','Compare fixed and folding laptop stands by keyboard access, stability, ventilation and the complete desktop footprint.','laptop stand adjustable','ノートパソコン スタンド', '''
<p>Raising a laptop also raises its built-in keyboard. Before buying a stand, decide whether you will type on that keyboard or use separate input devices. A stand can solve a screen-position problem while creating a new reach problem.</p>
<h2>Plan the complete arrangement</h2><p>Set a comfortable chair position and place the laptop where you can see it without leaning forward. If a raised screen works better, check where an external keyboard and mouse would fit. Include their cost and space in the decision. A stand is not a complete workstation by itself.</p>
<table><tr><th>Stand style</th><th>Useful scenario</th><th>What to check</th></tr><tr><td>Low wedge</td><td>Small angle change while using the built-in keyboard</td><td>Front lip pressure and typing stability</td></tr><tr><td>Raised platform</td><td>External keyboard and mouse setup</td><td>Base area, height and access to ports</td></tr><tr><td>Folding travel stand</td><td>Frequent packing</td><td>Locking mechanism and setup time</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical holder</td><td>Compatible closed-display workflow</td><td>Computer support, power and ventilation instructions</td></tr></table>
<h2>Size compatibility is more than screen inches</h2><p>Compare the computer's weight, depth and underside shape with the stand's published limits. Check where the support pads land and whether a front stop interferes with opening the lid. A compatible screen-size range does not tell you where a particular laptop's vents or rubber feet sit.</p>
<h2>Do not buy a cooling promise without evidence</h2><p>Choose a stand that respects the laptop manufacturer's ventilation guidance. An open design may leave vents clear, but that alone is not a measured temperature improvement. We do not provide cooling results or performance claims for untested stands.</p>
<h2>Test everyday movements</h2><ol><li>Open and close the screen with the stand on the intended desk.</li><li>Connect charging and display cables without straining the ports.</li><li>Type or use the external keyboard at a normal pace.</li><li>Check that the stand does not rock or slide.</li><li>Pack and unfold it if portability is the reason you bought it.</li></ol><p>Keep within the stand's adjustment and load limits. Do not improvise an unstable stack of objects to reach a preferred screen position.</p>
<h2>Shop the arrangement, not the metal finish</h2><p>A heavy stand may suit a permanent desk and be inconvenient in a daily bag. A compact travel stand may be less comfortable to adjust repeatedly. US and Japan listings can use different measurement units; compare the unfolded footprint as well as folded size. If the present setup works comfortably and a stand adds no clear benefit, keep the existing arrangement.</p>''')
add('travel-charger','A charger for a US–Japan desk and travel bag','Charging & cables','Read a charger label, compare single-port and shared output, and separate plug compatibility from voltage compatibility.','usb c charger multiport','USB C 充電器 複数 ポート', '''
<p>A charger that serves both a home desk and a travel bag needs to fit your devices, the destination's power supply and your packing routine. Start with the chargers you already own; a replacement is unnecessary if their labels and outputs already match the trip.</p>
<h2>Read the input label</h2><p>Japan uses 100 V AC. Check the exact charger's input voltage and frequency range against your destination. A compatible plug shape alone does not establish electrical compatibility. A plug adapter changes the physical connection; it does not convert voltage.</p>
<h2>List the devices used together</h2><p>Write down the laptop, phone and other devices you expect to charge at the same time. Consult each manufacturer's requirements. Then read the candidate charger's output table, including its multi-port combinations. The largest number in a listing headline may describe total output or a single-port mode rather than what your laptop receives with every port occupied.</p>
<table><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Benefit</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr><tr><td>Existing individual chargers</td><td>No new purchase and separate charging paths</td><td>More plugs and bag space</td></tr><tr><td>One multi-port charger</td><td>Fewer wall connections</td><td>Shared power and a single point of failure</td></tr><tr><td>Laptop charger plus small phone charger</td><td>Simple division of tasks</td><td>Two outlets may be needed</td></tr></table>
<h2>Check what happens when a second device connects</h2><p>Read whether the manufacturer documents power redistribution or a brief charging interruption. Check the exact port labels and supported charging protocols. A physically compatible connection is not a promise that your device's fastest advertised mode will work.</p>
<h2>Pack for the outlet, not just the suitcase</h2><p>A heavy charger may be awkward on a loose wall outlet. A short manufacturer-approved mains lead can be easier at some desks, but adds bulk. Check plug grounding requirements and never remove a ground pin to make a charger fit. Keep the charger uncovered and follow its safety instructions.</p>
<h2>US and Japan buying checklist</h2><ul><li>Verify input voltage and frequency on the actual regional model.</li><li>Confirm the included plug or mains cable.</li><li>Read the output table for your intended port combination.</li><li>Check cable ratings separately.</li><li>Compare local warranty, return and seller information.</li></ul><p>Do not use a travel charger plan for unrelated high-power appliances. If a label is missing or unreadable, obtain the manufacturer's instructions rather than guessing from an online photo. Buy capacity for the devices you use, not for imaginary future devices that make the charger larger and more expensive today.</p>''',[('JNTO: plugs and electricity in Japan','https://www.japan.travel/en/plan/plug-and-electricity/'),('NTT DOCOMO: checking travel charger input and plugs','https://www.docomo.ne.jp/service/world/roaming/check/battery/')])
add('desk-mat','Desk mats: measure the mouse area first','Desk setup','Pick a desk mat by working area, edge placement, cleaning needs and surface trade-offs instead of a staged setup photo.','desk mat mouse pad','デスクマット マウスパッド', '''
<p>A desk mat can make a mixed keyboard-and-mouse area feel more consistent, but it is not automatically an upgrade over a small mouse pad. Decide what you want it to do: support mouse movement, protect a writing area, or soften the sound of objects placed on the desk.</p>
<h2>Mark the footprint with paper</h2><p>Measure the space with the keyboard, mouse and notebook in their normal positions. Mark a proposed mat's corners with paper. Check that the mouse does not repeatedly cross an edge during normal work, and that the front seam will not sit directly beneath your wrist.</p>
<table><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Why consider it</th><th>What to verify</th></tr><tr><td>Cloth</td><td>Soft feel and a familiar mouse-pad texture</td><td>Cleaning instructions and drying time</td></tr><tr><td>Smooth wipeable surface</td><td>Easy routine wiping if the material permits</td><td>Mouse tracking, pen feel and care restrictions</td></tr><tr><td>Felt-style surface</td><td>A softer visual texture</td><td>Lint, friction and how the mouse moves</td></tr></table>
<h2>Match the care routine to your habits</h2><p>If coffee often sits beside the keyboard, read the material-specific cleaning instructions. Do not assume a mat is machine washable because another mat with a similar appearance is. Coatings, backing and adhesives can change what cleaning methods are suitable. Check whether the seller explains stain removal rather than merely calling the surface “easy care.”</p>
<h2>Watch the underside and edges</h2><p>A backing should keep the mat in place on your actual desk without harming its finish. Follow care guidance for both the furniture and mat, especially on delicate or recently refinished surfaces. A stitched edge may resist fraying but feel raised. A rolled mat may take time to flatten; check the manufacturer's instructions instead of using heat or heavy pressure without guidance.</p>
<h2>Use a return-window work test</h2><ol><li>Try small and broad mouse movements.</li><li>Write on a sheet of paper using your normal pen.</li><li>Check that the keyboard stays steady.</li><li>Inspect the front edge after an ordinary work session.</li><li>Confirm the mat does not cover anything that needs ventilation.</li></ol><p>For US listings, convert inches to centimetres before comparing Japanese alternatives. Compare the exact size and thickness in the selected variation. Large listing photos can make a modest mat look desk-sized.</p>
<h2>When a smaller pad is better</h2><p>If only the mouse needs a consistent surface, a smaller pad preserves more clear desktop and is easier to move for writing. Skip a full mat if it creates an awkward edge or makes cleaning harder. A coordinated colour scheme is optional; a surface that fits the way you work is the practical requirement.</p>''')
add('budget-home-office','Build a budget home office in the right order','Buying smarter','A staged home-office plan that prioritizes daily obstacles, existing equipment and complete purchase costs.','home office desk accessories','在宅勤務 デスク 用品', '''
<p>A budget home office works best when spending follows a real problem rather than a shopping list. Separate what prevents you from working from what merely looks unfinished. A coherent setup can grow slowly without buying matching accessories all at once.</p>
<h2>Audit one ordinary workday</h2><p>Write down the moments that interrupt a task: a phone cable falls behind the desk, a screen reflection hides text, the keyboard area is crowded, or an adapter lacks a required connection. Describe the problem without naming a product. This helps avoid buying a stand when the actual fix is moving a lamp.</p>
<h2>Use three spending stages</h2><table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Priority</th><th>Example action</th></tr><tr><td>Use what you own</td><td>Layout and basic usability</td><td>Clear unused objects and reposition the screen</td></tr><tr><td>Fix the daily obstacle</td><td>One verified limitation</td><td>Replace an unsuitable cable with a documented match</td></tr><tr><td>Refine the setup</td><td>Convenience after the basics work</td><td>Add a removable organizer only where needed</td></tr></table>
<h2>Budget for the complete solution</h2><p>A raised laptop stand may also require a keyboard and mouse. A lamp may need an adapter. A hub may need a separate cable or power supply. Compare the total required package, delivery and return terms rather than the headline item price. We do not list live prices because offers and bundles change.</p>
<h2>Keep a one-week purchase queue</h2><p>Note a potential upgrade and wait through several normal work sessions if the problem is not urgent. Record how often it would have helped. If you stop noticing the problem after rearranging the desk, remove the item from the queue. This makes a budget a decision tool rather than only a limit.</p>
<h2>Used equipment needs a different checklist</h2><p>For non-electrical accessories, check dimensions, missing parts and surface condition. For electrical items, readable ratings, a suitable regional supply and an identifiable manufacturer matter. A low purchase price does not justify damaged cables or unknown power equipment. Ask the seller about return rights and included parts before paying.</p>
<h2>Choose your shopping region deliberately</h2><p>If you live in Japan, a US listing may add shipping, import charges or difficult returns. If you live in the US, a Japan-market bundle may have different documentation or warranty coverage. Compare the local option first, then investigate imports only for a concrete advantage.</p>
<h2>Define “done”</h2><p>Your first setup is complete when you can carry out the normal task without a recurring avoidable interruption. It does not need every accessory pictured in a desk tour. Save the remaining budget for a need that appears through use, rather than inventing one to finish the photograph.</p>''')
add('buying-checklist','The ten-minute desk accessory buying check','Buying smarter','A reusable checkout checklist for dimensions, compatibility, regional bundles, seller details and the option to buy nothing.','desk accessories organizer','デスク アクセサリー', '''
<p>Before comparing star ratings or colours, define the job an accessory must do. A short written requirement keeps a convincing listing from changing your goal. This checklist works for stands, lights, cables, mats and organizers.</p>
<h2>Minute 1–2: name the task</h2><p>Write one sentence: “I need the phone to stay upright while charging during calls,” or “I need enough cable to reach the left side of my desk.” If the requirement is only “make the desk nicer,” decide how much you are comfortable spending on appearance before browsing.</p>
<h2>Minute 3–4: measure and verify</h2><p>Measure the footprint, cable route or clamp opening. For electronics, check the device maker's requirements and the accessory's exact model documentation. Do not rely on a listing that says only “universal.” Take account of cases, desk frames, nearby ports and cable bends.</p>
<table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Evidence to look for</th></tr><tr><td>Will it fit?</td><td>Measurements for the selected variation</td></tr><tr><td>Will it perform the task?</td><td>Documented power, data, load or mounting limits</td></tr><tr><td>Is the package complete?</td><td>Included-parts list, not just staged photos</td></tr><tr><td>Can I undo the choice?</td><td>Seller return terms and applicable conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Is it right for my region?</td><td>Regional model, power input and warranty details</td></tr></table>
<h2>Minute 5–6: compare total cost</h2><p>Include necessary adapters, cables and mounts. Check delivery and possible import costs for cross-border orders. A cheaper product that needs another purchase may not be cheaper in use. Confirm the seller and shipping details again after changing a colour, length or size option.</p>
<h2>Minute 7–8: read reviews for failure modes</h2><p>Use customer feedback to form questions, not to manufacture certainty. Look for reports relevant to your use, such as a hinge slipping or a case not fitting. Check whether reviews refer to the exact variation. Deskwise does not reproduce Amazon customer reviews or present them as our own testing.</p>
<h2>Minute 9–10: make a reversible decision</h2><p>Record the reason for the purchase and what a successful result will look like. Keep the receipt and appropriate packaging. When the item arrives, test the important job before spending time styling the desk around it. Follow return terms rather than assuming every opened item can be returned.</p>
<h2>The final question: can you skip it?</h2><p>Try one no-cost alternative: move an existing object, shorten a cable route, clear a drawer or change the work layout. If that resolves the problem, stop shopping. The best outcome of a buying guide is sometimes a purchase avoided.</p><p>For US and Japan comparisons, convert measurements and confirm the regional bundle. Similar names and photos do not prove identical products. Save the model number so that a later replacement can be checked accurately.</p>''')
add('us-japan-shopping','Amazon US or Japan: choose the store before the product','Buying smarter','Compare delivery, regional models, warranty and complete costs before shopping across Amazon USA and Japan.','desk accessories','デスク 用品', '''
<p>Amazon.com and Amazon.co.jp are separate storefronts. A familiar product name does not guarantee the same seller, package or after-sales support. Start with the country where you need delivery, then compare cross-border options only if there is a clear reason.</p>
<h2>Use the local listing as your baseline</h2><p>Check the local store for the exact product category and model. Note the measurements, included parts, seller and delivery estimate. This gives you a meaningful comparison when a foreign listing seems cheaper or carries a different model number.</p>
<table><tr><th>Compare</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr><tr><td>Exact model and variation</td><td>Similar names can hide different specifications</td></tr><tr><td>Delivered cost</td><td>Shipping and import-related charges can change the total</td></tr><tr><td>Power input and plug</td><td>Physical fit alone does not establish compatibility</td></tr><tr><td>Warranty territory</td><td>Service may depend on country or seller</td></tr><tr><td>Returns</td><td>International return shipping can be inconvenient</td></tr></table>
<h2>Translate the category, preserve the model number</h2><p>For Japan searches, try the local category phrase, such as スマホスタンド for phone stands or ノートパソコンスタンド for laptop stands. Then narrow with the exact model number. Automatic translation can help explain a listing, but use the manufacturer's documentation to resolve an important ambiguity.</p>
<h2>Compare complete bundles</h2><p>A charger may be sold with different cables or wall plugs. A light may include a supply in one bundle and require one separately in another. A pictured accessory may be a staging prop. Read the package contents for the variation you actually selected.</p>
<h2>Keep electrical checks separate</h2><p>Japan's standard supply is 100 V AC. Read the input voltage and frequency printed on the actual adapter. Do not infer compatibility from a two-blade plug or a similar product sold elsewhere. If the documentation does not answer the question, contact the manufacturer before purchase.</p>
<h2>Choose a buying region on purpose</h2><ol><li>Set the delivery destination on the store.</li><li>Find the exact local model and total required package.</li><li>Compare the foreign model's specifications and service terms.</li><li>Review the final checkout total in the store's currency.</li><li>Keep the order and model information for future support.</li></ol><p>Deskwise provides separate US and Japan search links so you can choose the destination yourself. The links currently contain no Associates tracking tags. We do not claim that either store is always cheaper or that products in search results have been individually checked.</p>
<h2>When importing is not worth it</h2><p>For a basic cable clip or mat with equivalent local options, a small sticker-price saving may not justify a complicated return. Cross-border shopping is more useful when a specific, verified feature is unavailable locally. Compare the practical outcome rather than treating a foreign listing as a bargain by default.</p>''',[('JNTO: electricity in Japan','https://www.japan.travel/en/plan/plug-and-electricity/')])
add('usb-c-hub-guide','USB-C hubs: build a port checklist first','Charging & cables','Plan a USB-C hub around host-port support, monitor requirements, power pass-through and simultaneous device use.','usb c hub hdmi power delivery','USB C ハブ HDMI PD', '''
<p>A hub can add useful connections, but it cannot give the host computer capabilities its port does not support. Before choosing a port count, make a list of what you need connected simultaneously: display, keyboard, storage, network and charger.</p>
<h2>Check the computer before the hub</h2><p>Find the documentation for the exact computer model and the port you intend to use. Check display-output support, charging support and any limits on external monitors. Two USB-C ports on the same computer may not be interchangeable for every task.</p>
<table><tr><th>Need</th><th>What to verify</th></tr><tr><td>External monitor</td><td>Host support and the hub's resolution/refresh limits for your setup</td></tr><tr><td>Laptop power</td><td>Supported pass-through input, output and required charger</td></tr><tr><td>External drive</td><td>Port data rate and any shared bandwidth limits</td></tr><tr><td>Wired network</td><td>Operating-system support and network capability</td></tr><tr><td>Daily portability</td><td>Cable length, connector strain and packed size</td></tr></table>
<h2>Read the fine print around displays</h2><p>A headline display specification may apply to one monitor or a particular connection mode. Check the manufacturer's compatibility information for your operating system and computer. Do not assume two physical video sockets mean two independent extended desktops on every machine.</p>
<h2>Power pass-through is a system decision</h2><p>If a hub accepts a charger, check how much power is available to the computer after the hub's own requirements. Verify the charger and cable as well as the hub. The pass-through rating alone does not promise that the computer will charge at its maximum rate during every task.</p>
<h2>Prefer the ports you will use</h2><p>More sockets can make a hub larger without improving your routine. A compact hub with the right display connection and two practical USB ports may be easier to carry than a dock full of unused interfaces. At a permanent desk, a separately powered dock may be worth considering when its documented capabilities match a more demanding setup.</p>
<h2>Test the whole routine together</h2><ol><li>Connect the charger, if supported.</li><li>Connect the intended monitor at your normal settings.</li><li>Attach storage and other peripherals.</li><li>Try your usual file transfer and display tasks together.</li><li>Check sleep, wake and reconnect behaviour.</li></ol><p>Follow the manufacturer's ventilation instructions and disconnect equipment if it behaves abnormally. Do not hang a heavy hub from a short laptop connector; place it on a supported surface.</p>
<h2>Before ordering in either marketplace</h2><p>Save the exact model number and compatibility page. Check whether a cable or adapter is included and whether support applies in your country. If the documentation does not resolve a crucial requirement, choose a clearer listing rather than assuming an attractive product photo proves compatibility.</p>''',[('DisplayPort: USB-C display questions','https://www.displayport.org/faq/'),('USB-IF: power delivery overview','https://usb.org/usb-charger-pd')])
def nav(prefix=''):
 return f'''<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header><div class="bar"><a class="brand" href="{prefix}index.html"><span class="brand-icon">d</span>deskwise<span class="brand-light">guide</span></a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="{prefix}index.html#guides">All guides</a><a href="{prefix}about.html">Our approach</a><a href="{prefix}contact.html">Contact</a></nav><span class="edition">US / JAPAN EDITION</span></div></header>'''
